{% set pillarBodyHtml %}
    <section data-aos="fade-up">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-xl font-light text-dark mb-4">The Definition</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        A third place is a space beyond home (the first place) and work or school (the second place) — somewhere you can simply be yourself. The term comes from sociologist Ray Oldenburg's 1989 book <em>The Great Good Place</em>, which described the informal, freely-entered spaces that give community its texture.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Third Place Japan (TPJ) builds on that theory with a seven-axis framework of its own, adapted for how space, silence, and belonging actually function in contemporary Japan — including the concept of <em>ibasho</em> (居場所), a place where one is simply allowed to be. See <a href="/en/stories/concept/what-is-third-place-japan/" class="text-gold hover:underline">What Is a Third Place? The TPJ Guide to Japan's Concept of Ibasho</a> for the full theory.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-aos="fade-up">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-xl font-light text-dark mb-4">The Eight Conditions</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Oldenburg identified eight conditions that tend to appear together in a genuine third place: neutral ground, leveling, conversation as the main activity, accessibility, regulars, a low profile, a playful mood, and a home away from home. None of these describe what a space sells — they describe how it behaves toward the people who return to it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Each condition is examined individually, with examples from contemporary Japan, in <a href="/en/stories/concept/third-place-8-conditions/" class="text-gold hover:underline">The Eight Conditions of a Third Place</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-aos="fade-up">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-xl font-light text-dark mb-4">TPJ's Seven-Axis Framework</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        TPJ scores every space it evaluates across seven axes: Comfort & Sensory Quality, Quietness & Privacy, Specialness & Non-daily Experience, Story & Empathy for Background, Revisit & Continuity Value, Record & Share Experience, and Inbound & Multilingual Compatibility.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        The seven axes fall into three groups — the quality of the space, its emotional core, and its wider reach — and function as one evaluation language across every category TPJ certifies. Each axis is broken down individually in <a href="/en/stories/concept/tpj-7-axis-evaluation-guide/" class="text-gold hover:underline">TPJ's Seven-Axis Evaluation Guide</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-aos="fade-up">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-xl font-light text-dark mb-4">Where the Framework Shows Up</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        A third place isn't limited to one kind of venue. A specialty coffee shop, a sento, a public library, a shrine forecourt, a coworking space, a suburban canal path — different as they are, each can satisfy the same underlying conditions.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        <a href="/en/stories/concept/third-place-examples/" class="text-gold hover:underline">Ten Kinds of Third Place</a> surveys how the concept plays out across ten distinct categories of space in Japan.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-aos="fade-up">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-xl font-light text-dark mb-4">TPJ Select and Certification</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        TPJ runs two separate systems on top of this framework. TPJ Select is an unpaid editorial pick — spaces our team finds and features, with no application involved. Certification (Certified, Silver, Gold, Platinum, Flagship) is a formal five-grade system a venue applies for and is assessed against.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
        <a href="/en/stories/concept/tpj-certification-and-select-difference/" class="text-gold hover:underline">TPJ Select vs. TPJ Certification</a> explains why the two tracks stay separate, and how a space can move from one into the other.
      </p>
    </section>
{% endset %}

